jj-musubi keeps your jujutsu (jj) repositories in sync across
machines, including work in progress. This lets you work on your
laptop, then pick up on another machine where you left off, without
needing to stop and commit. Syncing runs in the background.

One machine runs a hub, which stores shared history and coordinates
updates. A daemon on each machine that you do work on talks to the
hub over ssh to sync its local repos. A collection of synced copies
of a repo is a hive. One hub can serve many hives.

Engineers at Meta have similar workflows via Sapling's Commit Cloud,
and engineers at Google from an internal jj server. jj-musubi brings
these workflows to your own machines. It's similar to extending
jj's concept of a workspace to multiple machines.
